# ACMP backup schedule (P18b; cloud shape in P23).
#   CLOUD: installed for you — deploy/aws/08-bootstrap-box.sh runs `crontab -u root <this file>` on
#          every bootstrap. This file is the source of truth; `crontab -e` edits on the box are
#          DISCARDED on the next bootstrap, so make lasting changes here.
#   ON-PREM: still manual (runbooks/README.md step 6) —  crontab deploy/scripts/crontab.example
# Nightly full backup + every-4h business-day backups so data loss never exceeds 4h (NFR-056 RPO); RTO <= 8h
# (NFR-057) is met by restoring from the off-instance copy. backup.sh writes ONE .bak per stateful database
# (Acmp AND keycloak — ADR-0036 put Keycloak on the same SQL Server) into ACMP_BACKUP_DIR, then copies them to
# s3://$ACMP_BACKUP_BUCKET/sql/ via the instance profile (NFR-058: the copy must not share fate with the box).
# See deploy/runbooks/cloud-backup-dr.md.
#
# Adjust the path to your checkout. Output is appended to a log for after-the-fact detail.
#
# FAILURES ARE ALERTED BY backup.sh ITSELF, not by cron. This comment used to say a non-zero exit "is
# where an alert hooks in (mail, Seq webhook, etc.)" — a placeholder that was never filled, so a failed
# nightly backup was SILENT for as long as this file has existed. cron cannot help here: the lines below
# redirect stdout+stderr to a log, and Amazon Linux 2023 ships no MTA, so cron has nowhere to mail
# anything even without the redirect. backup.sh now publishes failures to ACMP_ALERT_TOPIC_ARN from an
# EXIT trap, and says so loudly in the log if that variable is unset or the publish is refused.

# ACMP_ENV_FILE IS LOAD-BEARING (DEF-022). Without it backup.sh defaults to deploy/.env, which exists
# on-prem but NOT in cloud — the bootstrap writes deploy/.env.cloud. The miss used to be swallowed, so
# ACMP_BACKUP_BUCKET stayed unset and the S3 copy was skipped while the run still reported success.
# backup.sh now refuses instead of falling through, but set it here anyway: the refusal is the net,
# not the plan. On-prem, point this at /opt/acmp/deploy/.env.
ACMP_ENV_FILE=/opt/acmp/deploy/.env.cloud

# Nightly full backup at 02:00
0 2 * * *      cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/backup.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1

# Every 4 hours during business days (Sun-Thu 08:00, 12:00, 16:00, 20:00) to hold RPO <= 4h
0 8,12,16,20 * * 0-4   cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/backup.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1

# AT BOOT (OQ-068 option (b), resolved 2026-08-09). The two schedules above only fire while the
# instance RUNS, and this environment's operating model deliberately stops it when idle (ADR-0034),
# so 02:00 and the 4-hourly slots are missed on almost every session and UAT is backed up only when
# somebody happens to have the box up at the right minute. This line converts "usually never" into
# "at least once per session" using the existing backup path unchanged. It does NOT deliver NFR-056's
# 4h RPO on a stopped-when-idle box — nothing on the box can, since cron cannot run while the
# instance is off. Production is always-on, where the schedules above hold on their own.
#
# THE SLEEP IS NOT A GUESS. backup.sh now ALERTS on failure (DEF-032), so firing before SQL Server is
# healthy would email a failure that is really just impatience — and an alert that cries wolf at every
# boot is the noise OQ-067 rejected. 10 minutes is this project's own budget for exactly this case:
# AC-082 gives a WARM stop/start 10 minutes to come back, which is what a reboot is. (The bootstrap's
# 15-minute deadline is a different measurement — a COLD box doing first-ever migration and realm
# import.) Past AC-082's budget the stack is genuinely late, so a failure alert is a TRUE positive.
# That is why this waits on a number rather than probing: the wait IS the assertion.
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, AND THE SLEEPS ARE WHAT ENFORCE IT — not the order of lines in this file.
# cron fires every @reboot entry at boot without sequencing them, so the only thing that makes the
# freshness check run BEFORE this backup is 120s vs 600s. Keep that gap. If this backup ran first,
# freshness would always pass, because the same boot just created the object it inspects — a check
# that passes for the wrong reason (PE-197). Freshness must report the state we LEFT the box in.
@reboot        cd /opt/acmp && sleep 600 && deploy/scripts/backup.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1

# FRESHNESS: answers "have backups STOPPED?", which the lines above cannot. backup.sh alerts when a
# run FAILS; a run that never HAPPENS produces no failure and no alert (OQ-068). @reboot matters more
# than the daily line here: on a box that is stopped when idle, boot is the moment somebody is present
# and able to act, and it is the only moment a check is guaranteed to run at all. Deliberately NOT a
# CloudWatch alarm — one would sit in ALARM permanently while the box is stopped, which is OQ-067's
# trap (an alarm in its expected state is one that gets ignored).
# Set ACMP_BACKUP_MAX_AGE_HOURS in the env file: ~26h for an always-on box, far higher for a box that
# is deliberately stopped for days, or the check becomes the noise it exists to avoid.
@reboot        cd /opt/acmp && sleep 120 && deploy/scripts/check-backup-freshness.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1
0 9 * * *      cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/check-backup-freshness.sh >> /var/log/acmp-backup.log 2>&1

# CONTAINER HEALTH (WBS-26.4 / SC-042, from DW-091). Answers "is anything sitting unhealthy RIGHT NOW?",
# which the boot assertions cannot ask because they only ever run at boot. up.sh's `up -d --wait` and
# 08-bootstrap-box.sh's wait_healthy both DO consume the healthcheck's verdict — at start-up. After that
# nothing observes a health transition: DEF-079 measured what `condition: service_healthy` cost (a 30s blip
# in SQL Server, Hangfire or object storage stops a dependent from starting at all), put it back to the
# operator, and they chose to keep the signal and drop the gate. The signal has had no reader since.
#
# ⛔ NO @reboot LINE, AND THE ABSENCE IS DELIBERATE — TWO SEPARATE REASONS. (1) Boot is the one moment the
# verdict IS already consumed, twice, so an @reboot check would duplicate a guard that exists. (2) Docker
# reports `starting` during a healthcheck's start_period and this script counts `starting` as NOT healthy —
# correct for steady state, a guaranteed false positive at boot. An alert that cries wolf every cold start
# is the noise OQ-067 rejected, and it would train the operator to ignore the one that matters.
#
# Every 15 minutes: fast enough that an unhealthy container is noticed within a quarter hour, slow enough
# that a genuine outage produces four notifications an hour rather than sixty. It emits a
# `Deploy.ContainerUnhealthy` CLEF event to Seq (the notification path that exists in BOTH topologies —
# on-prem has no SNS) and additionally publishes to ACMP_ALERT_TOPIC_ARN when one is configured.
*/15 * * * *   cd /opt/acmp && deploy/scripts/check-container-health.sh -f deploy/docker-compose.cloud.yml --env-file deploy/.env.cloud >> /var/log/acmp-health.log 2>&1
